citeturn6486view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({"ref_id":"turn6480view0","lineno":560}); Total lines: 807
L509: (2) Information flow: Reflexions successfully inform gradient computation (otherwise gradients would be computed in isolation), and gradients successfully guide reflexion priorities (otherwise reflexions would be generated independently).
L510: (3) Causal validation: When we artificially remove reflexions from gradient computation in a controlled experiment (not reported in main results), alignment drops to 81%, and success rate decreases to 52%, confirming that reflexion→gradient information flow is causally responsible for the observed synergy.
L511: ## 6 Experiments
L512: ### 6.1 Experimental Setup
L513: 
L514: Environment: We evaluate on ALFWorld [cite52†3 ], a text-based environment simulating household tasks. Agents interact through natural language actions (e.g., "go to cabinet 1", "take apple") and receive textual observations. Tasks include multi-step workflows: cooling objects, placing items, examining objects under light sources.
L515: 
L516: Benchmark: We construct a benchmark of 9 diverse environments spanning 3 task types:
L517: 
L518:   * •
L519: pick_cool_then_place_in_recep: Cool object and place in target location (3 environments)
L520: 
L521:   * •
L522: 
L523: pick_two_obj_and_place: Gather two objects and place in target location (3 environments)
L524: 
L525:   * •
L526: 
L527: look_at_obj_in_light: Examine object under light source (3 environments)
L528: 
L529: Each environment uses fixed seeds for reproducibility. Agents run for 2 trials (Trial 0 and Trial 1), enabling measurement of zero-shot performance and cross-trial learning.
L530: 
L531: Models: We employ a two-tier model architecture via OpenAI API:
L532:   * •
L533: 
L534: GPT-5 (Responses API with configurable reasoning): Primary model for strategic operations with adaptive reasoning levels:
L535: 
L536:     * –
L537: 
L538: Minimal reasoning ($\sim$100 tokens): Fast action selection
L539: 
L540:     * –
L541: 
L542: Medium reasoning ($\sim$1000 tokens): Reflexion generation and TextGrad computation
L543: 
L544:   * •
L545: 
L546: GPT-4o-mini: Lightweight model for fast auxiliary operations including TODO verification, memory compression, and TextGrad loss computation
L547: This hierarchical model allocation balances computational efficiency with reasoning quality. No fine-tuning is performed on either model.
L548: 
L549: Baselines: We compare against ablations:
L550: 
L551:   * •
L552: 
L553: Reflexion-Only: Episodic memory without TextGrad optimization
L554: 
L555:   * •
L556: 
L557: TextGrad-Only: Gradient-based optimization without episodic memory
L558: 
L559:   * •
L560: 
L561: Sequential Combination: Reflexion provides context to TextGrad without bidirectional coupling
L562: 
L563: Metrics:
L564: 
L565:   * •
L566: Success Rate: Percentage of environments successfully completed within episode horizon
L567: 
L568:   * •
L569: 
L570: Steps to Success: Number of actions required to complete task
L571: 
L572:   * •
L573: 
L574: Loop Count: Number of repeated failed actions (indicates inefficient exploration)
L575: 
L576:   * •
L577: 
L578: Alignment: Percentage of steps where TextGrad recommendation matches executed action
L579: ### 6.2 Main Results
L580: 
L581: Table cite78†1 presents our main experimental findings. We report Trial 0 (first exposure) results to emphasize zero-shot performance, then compare with few-shot baselines from prior work. Important note: Prior work reports aggregate performance across multiple trials with few-shot demonstrations, while we report Trial 0 performance without any demonstrations—a strictly harder setting.
L582: Table 1: ReflexGrad zero-shot Trial 0 performance compared with few-shot aggregate performance from prior work. Note: Baselines report overall performance across trials with demonstrations; ReflexGrad shows first-exposure zero-shot performance.
L583: Method  | Success Rate  | Demo Examples  | Loop Count  | Alignment
L584: Prior Work (Aggregate with Few-Shot)
L585: Reflexion [cite47†1 ]  | 91%  | 6-shot  | -  | -
L586: REBACT [cite73†29 ]  | 93%  | Few-shot  | -  | -
L587: ReflAct [cite74†30 ]  | 93%  | ICL  | -  | -
L588: A3T [cite75†31 ]  | 96%  | Few-shot + Fine-tune  | -  | -
L589: Our Work (Trial 0 Zero-Shot)
L590: ReflexGrad Trial 0  | 67%  | 0-shot  | 0  | 100%
L591: Key Findings:
L592: (1) Strong Zero-Shot First-Exposure Performance: ReflexGrad achieves 67% success on Trial 0 in a truly zero-shot setting (no demonstration examples, no task-specific training, no hardcoded ALFWorld knowledge). While few-shot baselines achieve higher aggregate performance (91-96%) across multiple trials with curated demonstrations, direct comparison is not straightforward—our Trial 0 zero-shot setting is strictly harder than aggregate few-shot performance.
L593: The result validates that architectural synergy enables competitive first-exposure performance without demonstrations.
L594: (2) Zero Action Loops: Unlike baseline approaches that exhibit repeated failed actions, ReflexGrad achieves zero loops through TODO-guided exploration and history-aware reflexion generation. The TODO system provides strategic structure that prevents aimless wandering, while reflexions enable detection of repetitive failures.
L595: (3) Perfect Triple Synergy: 100% TODO-TextGrad-Reflexion alignment across all steps indicates effective three-way coupling. TODOs structure reflexion analysis, reflexions inform gradient computation, and gradients guide both TODO progression and action selection.
L596: (4) Zero-Shot Task Understanding: Without any demonstration examples, the system successfully decomposes diverse tasks into appropriate subgoals, generates meaningful reflexions, and optimizes prompts—demonstrating that LLM semantic reasoning alone can enable task understanding.
L597: (5) Effective Cross-Trial Learning: Performance improves from 67% (Trial 0, zero-shot) to 78% (Trial 1), demonstrating that episodic reflexions successfully transfer causal insights across trials. This 11 percentage point improvement validates the effectiveness of our LLM-based semantic memory retrieval for knowledge reuse.
L598: ### 6.3 Ablation Studies
L599: 
L600: To isolate the contribution of each component and validate the synergistic integration, we compare ReflexGrad against ablated variants. Critically, all ablations are evaluated in the identical zero-shot setting as ReflexGrad—no few-shot demonstrations, no task-specific context, no hardcoded knowledge—ensuring fair comparison.
L601: 
